# w-5e2d41 — is a cheap-end retention floor sufficient to keep `hid` in the pool?

Front: measurement, evidence and specification only. `src/lib.rs` is read-only
in this worktree. Nothing is re-pinned, no baseline is changed, and the two
surfaces this item is adjacent to — the shortlist fill ([w-5e2d41](w-5e2d41.md)
proper, agent-5e2d411) and the reserve sweep (w-c3f81a) — are **not touched**.

Recommendation: **the dichotomy the item offers is false in both directions**,
and that is the finding. A cheap-end retention floor is **not** sufficient, and
the global 16,384-emission budget is **not** the binding constraint. The
constraint that actually decides the guard is the per-slot opening width
`affordable_opening_width(d, 4_000)` — 5 for six slots, 7 for five — plus the
depth-profile reserve's 16 strided draws per segmentation. The guard is
nevertheless **reachable by a rule on which shortlist candidates are retained**
(w-5e2d41 surface), because the decisive reserve draw is bit-identical on the
two heads and the only difference is one word's membership of one span's
shortlist. Details and arithmetic in §3 and §4.

