So: **`a_lattice_alignment_can_be_absent_from_the_production_pool` is the one
guard `a8a8f70` turns red, and all three `approximate_pool_reaches_*` guards
stay green.** The fill did not cost the pool-reach properties. That is the
matrix the next pass needs.

### 1.1 One correction to the predecessor's reading of the same failure

[w-7b40d2](w-7b40d2.md) §6 reports the five canonical words as "all five still
in the shortlist of the span each lands on". Measured per span, that is true
for four and false for one, and the exception is the whole story:

| word | pool wordings, base | pool wordings, head |
|---|---|---|
| `hits` | 19 | 18 |
| `justice` | 10,032 | 10,029 |
| `dupe` | 25 | 23 |
| **`hid`** | **2** | **0** |
| `came` | 135 | 135 |

(canonical case-2 pool: 18,936 → 18,933 clues.)

`hid` is the only word whose pool presence changes qualitatively, from 2
wordings to 0. And `hid` is **not** in the shortlist of every span it is
offered on at `a8a8f70` — at the public API boundary,
`Generator::span_shortlists`, target `It's just a stupid game`:

| word | span | offered | cost | cost-rank | in shortlist at `a8a8f70` |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `hid` | `[0,2)` | 256 | 0.3500000000 | 117 | **no** |
| `hid` | `[2,4)` | 256 | 0.3615549442 | 63 | yes |
| `hid` | `[3,4)` | 256 | 0.4000000000 | 218 | no |
| `hid` | `[13,15)` | 256 | 0.3695198142 | 105 | yes |
| `hid` | `[14,15)` | 256 | 0.4000000000 | 218 | no |
| `hits` | `[0,3)` / `[0,4)` / `[1,3)` | 256 / 98 / 256 | 0.2 / 0.4 / 0.4 | 9 / 15 / 141 | yes (all three) |
| `justice` | 13 spans, `[1,10)`…`[5,10)` | 1…63 | 0.0–0.4 | 1–21 | yes (all 13) |
| `dupe` | `[9,13)`…`[11,14)` | 38…256 | 0.15–0.4 | 4–120 | yes (all 6) |
| `came` | `[14,19)`…`[17,19)` | 35…256 | 0.2–0.4642 | 10–154 | yes (all 5) |

So the sentence that matters is narrower than "all five are in the shortlist":
**every canonical word is retained on the spans that carry it into a retained
segmentation; `hid` is not retained on `[0,2)`, and that one span is where its
last pool wordings went.**

## 2. Which span each word lands on, and where it is lost

### 2.1 The canonical five-word alignment is absent from the pool on **both** heads

Worth stating because it re-scopes the fence: the test's *first* assertion —
that the alignment `["hits","justice","dupe","hid","came"]` is not in the
production pool — **passes on base too**. What `a8a8f70` breaks is the
*second* assertion, that each of the five words is individually present. The
red guard is a statement about one **word**, not about the five-word
combination.

Enumerating every span chain that could carry the alignment (it must start at
0, assign one span per word in order, and end at 19; 8 such chains exist, all
of them using only spans on which all five words are retained), from the
instrumented DP:

| candidate chain | base `3e821c3` | head `a8a8f70` |
|---|---|---|
| `[(0,3),(3,8),(8,13),(13,15),(15,19)]` | completed at rank 300, cut by `SEGMENTATION_KEEP` | completed at rank 300, cut by `SEGMENTATION_KEEP` |
| `[(0,3),(3,9),(9,13),(13,15),(15,19)]` | completed at rank 284, cut by `SEGMENTATION_KEEP` | completed at rank 284, cut by `SEGMENTATION_KEEP` |
| `[(0,3),(3,10),(10,13),(13,15),(15,19)]` | **retained** (rank 151) | **retained** (rank 151) |
| `[(0,3),(3,11),(11,13),(13,15),(15,19)]` | **retained** (rank 187) | **retained** (rank 187) |
| `[(0,3),(3,12),(12,13),…]` | impossible (`dupe` not offered on `[12,13)`) | same |
| the three `[(0,4),…]` variants | never completed | never completed |

So the alignment **is** a retained segmentation on both heads, at the same rank
and the same position in the emission schedule. It is not the DP's fault and it
is not `SEGMENTATION_KEEP`'s.

### 2.2 The three candidate mechanisms, decided one at a time

**(a) A `min_cost` tightening that prunes the path — NO.** The structural DP
prunes a path in two places: `min_cost(path) + min_cost(edge) > total_budget`,
and the admissible `span_bound(edge.end, …) < incumbent`. Instrumented counts
over every path that touches a canonical word, both heads:

| event | base | head |
|---|---|---|
| `PRUNE-COST` (min_cost over `total_budget`, or `max_words`) | 284 | 294 |
| **`PRUNE-BOUND` (admissible bound under the incumbent)** | **0** | **0** |

Zero on both. And the extremum that would have moved is bit-identical: the
retained `min_cost` of the span that carries `hid` in the decisive structure,
`[13,15)`, is **0.1012336003** on base and on head, and the width is 160 on
both. The retained `SEGMENTATION_KEEP` ranks are the same list on both heads
(71, 122, 126, 135 for the four `hid`-bearing chains). The fill changed
*which* 160 are retained, not the bound the DP prunes with, and the DP pruned
this path zero times on both.

**(b) A change of branch composition — NO, for the word.** The 6-slot structure
that actually emitted `hid` on base,
`[(0,2),(2,3),(3,10),(10,13),(13,15),(15,19)]`, is retained on both heads at
the **same** `SEGMENTATION_KEEP` rank (126), funded at the **same** schedule
position (126), with the **same** widths `[160,160,7,160,160,93]`, the **same**
opening cap (5), the **same** per-segmentation allowance (64) and the **same**
reserve draw count (16 of 16). Branch composition is identical where it
matters.

**(c) A shortfall of the global emission budget — NO.** Measured at the decisive
structure, from the `FUND` line: `budget_left = 7,312` of 16,384 — **44.6 % of
the global emission budget was still unspent** when `hid`'s wording was emitted
on base. The budget is exhausted only at **schedule position 240 of 256**, long
after. Pop budget at exhaustion: 175,957 of 1,024,000 (**17.2 %**). Both heads:
`spent_em = 16384/16384`, `BUDGET-EXHAUSTED at sched pos 240`. Nothing about
the 16,384 is what removed `hid`.

### 2.3 The actual mechanism, named

The traversal cannot reach `hid` at all, and neither can the reserve by
accident. The five words' positions in their slots, in the two retained
alignments (head, from the `SLOT` lines; `cap` is the opening width the walk is
allowed to read):

| structure | sched | cap | `hits` | `justice` | `dupe` | `hid` | `came` |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `[(0,3),(3,10),(10,13),(13,15),(15,19)]` | 151 | 7 | 7 | 0 | 13 | **119** | 11 |
| `[(0,3),(3,11),(11,13),(13,15),(15,19)]` | 187 | 7 | 7 | 0 | 22 | **119** | 11 |

`cap = affordable_opening_width(5, 4_000) = 7` (because
`1+7+7²+7³+7⁴ = 2,801 ≤ 4,000 < 4,681 = 1+8+…+8⁴`). Four of the five indices
are ≥ 7, so the prefix walk can never emit this alignment on either head. The
stage-widening path (`next_branch_stage`, 7→28→112→160) is the designed escape,
and it is **dead on this target**: instrumented `STAGE-DRAIN` events, whole
run, both heads: **0**. A stage only widens after the walk *drains* the heap,
and these walks never drain — they stop on the per-segmentation allowance, which
the depth-profile reserve has already taken 14–16 of, leaving 48–50 of 64, and
the walk fills exactly that (`EMITTED … emitted=50 profile=14`, `emitted=48
profile=16`).

**The consumer that spends the slot `hid` needs is the depth-profile reserve** —
`coverage_tuples` with `EMIT_PROFILE_RESERVE = 16` and
`EMIT_PROFILE_MAX_DEEP = 3`, drawn once per segmentation at the head of that
segmentation's allowance. It is the only mechanism in the search that reads a
slot index past the opening cap. All sixteen of its draws at the decisive
structure, both heads:

| draw | base `3e821c3` | head `a8a8f70` |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **`[136,0,0,0,0,0]` → slot 0 = `hid`** | **`[136,0,0,0,0,0]` → slot 0 = *not* `hid`** |
| 2 | `[0,146,0,0,0,0]` | `[0,146,0,0,0,0]` |
| 3 | `[0,0,0,10,0,0]` → `justice`, `dupe` | `[0,0,0,10,0,0]` → `justice`, `dupe` |
| 4–10 | identical | identical |
| 11 | `[0,87,0,0,100,0]` | `[0,86,0,0,93,0]` |
| 12–16 | identical | identical |

Draw 1 is **bit-identical** on the two heads. On base, slot 0 of that
structure — span `[0,2)` — held `hid` at rank **136 of 160**; on head it does
not hold `hid` at all, and rank 136 is some other word. **That single cell is
the whole difference.** The other base wording containing `hid` used slot 4
(span `[13,15)`), where `hid` sits at rank 99 on base and rank 123 on head; that
one *is* additionally sensitive to the reserve's sampled pick (draw 11 moved
87→86 and 100→93, because the sampled draw is chosen by the admissible `bound`,
which the fill changes), but it is not needed: one retained occurrence is
enough for the guard.

## 3. Is a cheap-end retention floor sufficient? — **No, and it is already vacuous**

The floor the item proposes is the shape w-7b40d2 §6.2 argues for: *retain at
least as many candidates at or below each cheap cost threshold as the global
`quality` order would have retained, then apportion the rest.* Measured at the
public API boundary on the head, per span of the canonical target, with the
same 1.5×-of-cheapest threshold w-0f3a17 and w-7b40d2 use
(`Generator::span_shortlists` vs `span_candidates`, `per_word_budget = 0.5`):

**On all 108 spans of `It's just a stupid game`, `cheap1.5x_retained ==
cheap1.5x_offered`. Zero spans drop a candidate inside their own 1.5×-of-minimum
band.** That is w-7b40d2's 623 → 0, restated per span, and it means the floor
is **already satisfied everywhere on this head** — a rule that only constrains
the cheap end cannot change anything here, because the cheap end is already
complete. It is not that the floor is too weak; on this head it has no work to
do.

And it could not reach `hid` even if it did, by arithmetic. On the span that
matters, `[0,2)`:

* the span's `min_cost` is **0.0000000000**;
* `hid` is offered at cost **0.3500000000**;
* so the cheap-end *ratio* `0.35 / 0.0` is **not finite**: a multiplicative
  floor of any multiple `k` retains exactly the `⌊k · 0⌋ = 0`-band, i.e. the
  single zero-cost candidate, and `hid` is outside every `k`. The 1.5× band of
  this span is **1 of 256** offered candidates and `hid` is not in it.

The floor family is therefore wrong in *form* for this guard. What `hid` needs
is a floor on a **band that is not the cheapest one**, or an absolute/rank
floor — retaining some candidates at cost ≈ 0.35 in a span whose minimum is
0.0. Note this is the same shape as w-7b40d2 §6.2's own arithmetic on
`taco cat` `[6,8)`, where the counterexample bound the *thickest* band, not the
cheapest: `[0,2)` is the extreme case, a span whose floor sits at zero, so the
"cheap end" is a single word and everything else is unconstrained by it.

**So: FLOOR-SUFFICIENT is false, and the negative answer is the deliverable.**
It is recorded plainly rather than made to pass.

## 4. Is the 16,384-emission budget the binding constraint? — **No**

Measured, not argued:

* at the structure that emits `hid`, **7,312 of 16,384 emissions (55.4 % spent)
  are unspent**; the ceiling is reached only at schedule position **240 of 256**;
* the pop ceiling, the one that actually bounds wall clock, is at **17.2 %**
  (175,957 of 1,024,000) — `LEXICAL_GLOBAL_POP_BUDGET` is not close to binding;
* the per-structure depth account shows the real shape: **256 structures, 256
  schedules, one structure each**, `structure_depth_ceiling` giving each
  `LEXICAL_COMBINATIONS_PER_SEGMENTATION = 64`, of which the reserve takes
  14–16 and the traversal spends the remaining 48–50 and stops. The traversal
  is **allowance-bound, not budget-bound**;
* the traversal is also **width-bound**: `STAGE-DRAIN` fires 0 times in the
  whole run on both heads, so no widening from 5/7 towards 160 ever begins, and
  raising any global ceiling would not reach a slot index of 119.

The number that decides it: **7,312 unspent emissions of 16,384 at the point of
loss** — a 1.86× headroom against the very budget the item offers as the
explanation. **BUDGET-BINDING is false as well.**

### 4.1 The property a successor would need instead, with arithmetic

If a successor wants a *general* statement that makes the pool-content guard
hold without naming a word, the honest candidate is **not** a cheap-end floor
and **not** a bigger global budget. It is a statement about the *pair*
(shortlist contents, reserve draws):

> For every span and every candidate `c` the shortlist retains, `c` must be
> reachable at the traversal's opening width **or** at an index the reserve
> sweep is guaranteed to draw — and a 16-draw systematic sample of a
> `160·160·7·160·160·93 = 4.266·10¹¹`-cell product grid is a cover of each
> *slot*, never of the *tuple*, so no property of the form "every retained
> candidate is drawn" exists at this budget.

The arithmetic that makes the pair necessary and the two halves individually
insufficient:

* **Width half.** For a `d`-slot structure the walk reads indices
  `0..min(cap, width)` with `cap = affordable_opening_width(d, 4_000)`, i.e.
  `1 + cap + … + cap^{d-1} ≤ 4_000`. Measured: `cap = 10` at `d ≤ 4`, `7` at
  `d = 5`, `5` at `d = 6`. A candidate at rank `r` in a slot is unreachable
  unless `r < cap`; the canonical alignment needs `r = 7, 0, 13, 119, 11`, so
  it is unreachable at `d = 5` and no budget change alters that — only
  `cap`, `SPAN_SHORTLIST` (the shortlist *width*, 160) or the stage-widening
  trigger can.
* **Sweep half.** The reserve's coverage argument
  (`sweep_index`, `w-c3f81a`) is that one sweep *tiles each slot's list*:
  `stride = span.div_ceil(per)` with a rotated start. That is a per-slot
  statement. It says nothing about a 5- or 6-dimensional tuple, and the
  measured draws show it: 15 of the 16 draws at the decisive structure are
  identical across the two heads while the *contents* at one index differ, and
  one draw moved (87→86, 100→93) purely because the sampled candidate is
  picked by the admissible `bound`, which the fill perturbs.
* Consequently the only rule that can restore this guard, given a fixed sweep
  and a fixed `cap`, is a rule on **which candidates a span retains and at what
  rank** — which is the w-5e2d41 surface — and it must be stated as a *rank*
  or *band* floor, not a cheap-end ratio (§3).

**Clean yes/no for the coordinator's split-or-merge question: the consumer is
reachable by a rule on which shortlist candidates are retained — the
**w-5e2d41** surface (agent-5e2d411) — and not only by a rule on the reserve
sweep placement (w-c3f81a).** The decisive reserve draw `[136,0,0,0,0,0]` at
schedule position 126 is *bit-identical* on the two heads; only the shortlist
of span `[0,2)` changed under it. w-c3f81a's surface is therefore **not** on
the critical path for this guard, and a successor should not spend a front on
it for this fence. (It *is* on the critical path for the **second** base
wording, via draw 11 and the 99→123 rank shift — recorded, not needed.)

## 5. Scope and hygiene

* **No `src/` edit reaches any branch.** `src/lib.rs` in this worktree is
  byte-identical to `a8a8f70` (`git status` clean except the two files added
  below). The instrumentation exists only in `/workspace/floor-5e2d42-probe`
  and `/workspace/floor-5e2d42-baseprobe`, and is published as a **patch
  file** under `docs/work/`, not as a source change.
* No re-pinning, no baseline change, no `#[cfg(test)]` counter, no `ZZ_*`
  instrumentation in the production tree, no environment knob introduced.
* `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy` and doctests **cannot run on this host** (see
  [../environment-notes.md](../environment-notes.md)) and are not claimed. Only
  `cargo test --release --test <name>` was used as verification.
* Files added by this front: `docs/work/items/w-5e2d41.md` (this record),
  `docs/work/w-5e2d42-measurement-probe.patch`,
  `docs/work/w-5e2d42-probe-example.rs`.
* No third front was opened. Neither w-5e2d41's implementation front nor
  w-c3f81a was touched.

## 6. One-line verdict

**NEITHER — the deciding numbers are `hid` at cost 0.3500000000 against a span
`min_cost` of 0.0000000000 on `[0,2)` (a 1.5× cheap-end floor is already
satisfied on 108 of 108 spans, so it is vacuous), and 7,312 of 16,384 emissions
unspent at the point of loss (1.86× headroom), with the traversal stopped by
`affordable_opening_width(5, 4_000) = 7` against a needed rank of 119.**

