# Front A (agent-5e2d411) — implementation attempt, priced negative

Branch `madgab-floor-5e2d41`, worktree `/workspace/madgab-floor-5e2d41`, base
`a8a8f70`. **No `src/` line reaches this branch**: `src/lib.rs` is byte-identical
to `a8a8f70` (`git diff a8a8f70 -- src/` is empty). The floor was implemented,
measured and then abandoned on front B's refutation, which my own measurements
had already independently reproduced. The implementation survives only as
commits `f2fb62e` (WIP snapshot, pushed for durability) and `8a1aea2` (its
revert), so the work is auditable and lands nothing.

Recommendation: **HOLD**. The floor is not merely insufficient for this guard,
it is *vacuous at the threshold the item names* and it is *not free* where it
does bind.

## A1. What I measured first, on `a8a8f70`, before editing anything

`Generator::span_candidates` / `span_shortlists` at `per_word_budget = 0.5`,
twelve real targets, 1.5×-of-span-cheapest criterion, same as w-7b40d2 §1/§4:

| | base `3e821c3` (w-7b40d2) | `a8a8f70`, reproduced here |
|---|---|---|
| span edges | 939 | **939** |
| cheap candidates within 1.5× of their span cheapest | 7,056 | **7,051** |
| of those, dropped at every width | 623 (8.8 %) | **0** |
| worst per-span share | 24 of 56 (43 %) | **0 of 1** |
| unrepresented non-empty cost bands (3,234 surveyed) | 1 | **0** |
| unrepresented bands within 1.5× of cheapest (1,371 surveyed) | 2 | **0** |

Both canonical rank-50 cutoffs, `--top 50`, pool API, unchanged at
**0.918796440893** (`recognize speech`) and **0.915121574454**
(`It's just a stupid game`). The red fence reproduced exactly:
`emit_coverage` **3 passed / 1 failed**, the one failure
`a_lattice_alignment_can_be_absent_from_the_production_pool` on the word
`"hid"`.

## A2. The floor is vacuous at 1.5×, and the item's own threshold is 1.5×

Sweeping the multiplier outward over all 885 multi-candidate spans, counting
spans whose cheap set is *not fully retained* and the total shortfall:

| multiplier of span cheapest | spans unsaturated (of 885) | total shortfall |
|---|---|---|
| **1.5×** | **0** | **0** |
| 2.0× | 84 | 5,379 |
| 2.25× | 89 | 6,371 |
| 3.375× | 165 | 12,785 |
| 8.0× | 269 | 23,004 |

A floor at the threshold w-0f3a17 and w-7b40d2 both use has **no work to do on
this head**: 0 spans unsaturated, 0 candidates short. This is front B's §3
finding, reached independently by sweep rather than by case analysis, and it is
the reason no implementation of the floor as specified can move this fence: a
constraint that is already satisfied everywhere cannot change any output.

## A3. Why no cost-ordered rule reaches the needed rank — the arithmetic

A rank-based reading of the same failure, which is the part that decides what a
successor must do. The canonical five-word alignment needs slot-0 indices
`7, 0, 13, 119, 11`; on the base head its `hid` sits at **rank 136** of slot 0
and on `a8a8f70` it is not in slot 0 at all. The traversal for a `d`-slot
structure reads indices `0..min(cap, width)` with
`cap = affordable_opening_width(d, 4_000)`, i.e. `1 + cap + … + cap^{d-1} ≤ 4_000`,
which measures `cap = 10` at `d ≤ 4`, **`7` at `d = 5`**, `5` at `d = 6`.

So the needed rank **119** (and the base rank 136) is **17× and 19× past the
opening width of 7** at the five-slot structure where the alignment is emitted.
Three consequences, each measured rather than argued:

* **A global budget change cannot reach it.** Raising the emission ceiling
  changes how *many* tuples are drawn, not how deep into a slot the traversal
  looks; front B measures 7,312 of 16,384 emissions unspent at the point of loss.
* **A cost-ordered retention rule cannot reach it either, and this is the part
  specific to my front.** Cost ordering is exactly the ordering under which the
  cheap end saturates first: at 1.5× the cheap set is *already* complete
  (A2), so a cost rule can only permute which cheap words occupy ranks `0..6`,
  and `hid` is not one of them — on span `[0,2)` the span minimum is 0.0 and
  `hid` sits at cost 0.35, so **no finite multiple of the minimum reaches it**,
  and it is outside every cheap band of that span. Any rule of the form
  "retain more of the cheap end" therefore either (a) is satisfied already, or
  (b) reorders words none of which is the one missing.
* **What is needed is a rank or non-cheapest-band property, not a cost-ratio
  one** — a guarantee about *which* candidates occupy the first `cap` ranks of
  each slot, stated over coverage or structural distinctness rather than over
  cost.

## A4. The floor is not free: binding it costs band representation

This is my front's own contribution and it is a second, independent reason not
to land the floor. The floor's demand has to be *funded*: the list is 160 wide,
so retaining more cheap-end candidates means releasing dearer ones. I
implemented the funding three ways and measured the cost of each.

The cheap end is retained by the apportionment *for free*, because the integral
apportionment exhausts a **thin** band before a thick one — which is precisely
why criterion 2 is 0 without any floor. The floor therefore only ever binds on
**mid-cost, thick** bands, and it is funded from the **dear** end. Measured
consequences of letting it bind, with a derived 4-tier ladder:

* **Unconstrained funding drains the top of the cost range.** On span `(8,10)`
  of `It's just a stupid game` the retained maximum cost fell to **0.400000**
  against an offered maximum of **0.499816**, emptying the test's cost band 3
  of 4 (27 offered, 0 retained) — `every_non_empty_cost_band_contributes_to_the_span_shortlist`
  goes **red**, i.e. the floor *breaks the predecessor's own guard*. Across the
  3,234-band survey: 1–3 unrepresented bands for every binding depth tried.
* **Protecting band and tier last-representatives, and refusing to fund from
  the dearest tier at all, restores 0 unrepresented** and keeps criterion 2 at 0
  — and then the floor is nearly free but also nearly **inert**: the fence stays
  **red**, exactly as A2 predicts.

## A5. The parameter-free limit of the floor family is the global fill

A structural reason the depth of the ladder cannot simply be "made large".
State the floor as w-7b40d2 §6.2 does — at *each* cheap cost threshold, retain
at least as many candidates at or below it as the global `quality` order would —
and let the threshold set be every candidate cost. Then at the dearest retained
cost of the global order the demand is `|global| = min(160, offered)`, which on
any span offering more than 160 candidates is **the entire list**. So the
parameter-free member of this family **is** the global `quality` fill: it
reproduces w-7b40d2's pre-stratification state and re-opens the 623. The family
therefore has **no** parameter-free member that is both a strict constraint and
compatible with stratification, and the width of the "cheap" restriction is a
real trade knob, not a free parameter.

I measured where that knob sits against the fence, and record it as a **negative
with a caveat attached rather than as a setting**: ladders of 1.5×4, 1.4×8,
1.3×10 and 1.2×14 leave `emit_coverage` red; 1.25×12 and 1.1×20 turn it green.
The second group was found **by scanning the depth against the fence**. That is
fitting a constant to the fence even with the constant documented, and it is not
landed. It is recorded here only so the coordinator can see the shape of the
trade and does not re-derive it.

For completeness, the safe derived form was implemented rather than left as an
assertion: the ladder resolved into as many geometric cost tiers as the rule's
own cost axis has bands (`SPAN_COST_BANDS = 4`, the same resolution the axis
already uses to say what cost differences are distinguishable), funded only from
the cheap and middle tiers, never releasing a band or tier's last
representative. Under it the shortlist is genuinely re-selected and not a no-op
— **90,829 retained entries over the twelve targets on both heads, identical
width, 9,862 → 9,864 distinct words** — criterion 2 stays at **0 dropped**, the
band surveys stay at **0 unrepresented**, both canonical cutoffs are unmoved,
and `emit_coverage` is **still 3 passed / 1 failed**. That is the whole result
in one line: a correct, derived, cheap-end floor re-selects the list without
narrowing it and does not turn the guard green.

## A6. Successor rule, one line

> Replace the cheap-end **cost-ratio** floor with a **rank/band floor on the
> traversal's opening width**: for every span, the first
> `cap = affordable_opening_width(d, 4_000)` candidates of each slot must span
> the span's own candidate set in *coverage* or *structural distinctness* — not
> in cost — so a word needed at rank 119 is either inside the opening width or
> provably not needed there.

